   "l": "The Way, a void — and yet using it, somehow it is never full.\nSo deep! — it seems the ancestor of the ten-thousand things.\nIt blunts their sharp points,\nunties their tangles,\nbrings their light into accord,\nmakes itself one with their dust.\nSo still! — it seems perhaps to be there.\nI do not know whose child it is;\nit is like what comes before the Lord.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The Way is empty, and yet you draw on it and somehow it is never filled. So deep — it seems the ancestor of the ten thousand things. It takes the edge off their sharp points, loosens their snarls, brings their brightness into accord, makes itself one with their dust. So still — it seems, perhaps, to be there at all. Whose child it is I do not know; it is like something that came before the Lord himself.",
   "n": "兮 is the sigh of the old southern verse — a held beat rather than a word — and it marks 淵 and 湛 as exclamations: 'so deep!', 'so still!'. 沖 is generally taken as standing for the word written 盅, which the Shuowen dictionary (2nd c. AD) defines as 'a vessel, hollow' and illustrates with this very line of Laozi: empty the way a bowl is empty. That identification is a commentators' reading, not something the character itself tells you. Notice how little this chapter is willing to assert: 或 'somehow, perhaps' twice, 似 'it seems' twice, 象 'it resembles' at the end. The four middle lines are a real crux. 其 may be 'its' — the Way's own edge and glare — or 'their', belonging to the ten thousand things; and the four lines are read by some as instructions to the reader rather than description, because they return word for word in chapter 56, where the subject is plainly a person. We take them here as description of the Way and flag the doubt. 帝 is not an abstract deity: it is Di, the high god of the Shang kings, the highest thing there was to name. To say the Way seems to come before him is the boldest line in the chapter.",
   "p": true
  }
 ],
